<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean de Léry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1536">1536</a>–<a href="/years/1613">1613</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean de Léry was a French Protestant pastor and writer who was one of the Calvinists sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> to the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> Antarctique in the bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1557. After quarrelling with the governor Villegaignon, he lived for some months among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupinamba">Tupinambá</a>. His History of a Voyage to the Land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, published in 1578, became a classic of early ethnography and travel writing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-de-lery.jpg" alt="Jean de Léry" width="293" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean de Léry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brasilia2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
